            {
                "entry_id": "bfbd9eee-a183-330a-83ae-384768f5a810",
                "word": "miscellanist",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "40e00d19-59d1-430f-9e09-12311d07442c",
                        "entry_id": "bfbd9eee-a183-330a-83ae-384768f5a810",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "miscellanists"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4563957a-8357-3ab3-981d-00a5ec22b330",
                        "entry_id": "bfbd9eee-a183-330a-83ae-384768f5a810",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An author or editor of one or more miscellanies; one who produces written works having a wide range of forms or kinds of content."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6656db14-d3c9-4ace-a7c3-1f26b2d2a758",
                                "sense_id": "4563957a-8357-3ab3-981d-00a5ec22b330",
                                "text": "Leigh Hunt tried almost every conceivable kind of literature, including a historical novel. […] All this we may not unkindly brush away, and consider him first as a poet, secondly as a critic, and thirdly as what can be best, though rather unphilosophically, called a miscellanist."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5a69bfd-1bbe-4ac8-aa6a-dbae7fedc7ce",
                                "sense_id": "4563957a-8357-3ab3-981d-00a5ec22b330",
                                "text": "Professor Sadler relied heavily on a voluminous work (still unfinished, although some 60 volumes have already been published) by Tokutomi Iichiro, History of the Japanese People in Modern Times. Unfortunately, this Japanese journalist, a prolific writer and a miscellanist, is not accepted as a historian among scholars."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dbd14510-1026-4f28-9711-f491aeee4e35",
                                "sense_id": "4563957a-8357-3ab3-981d-00a5ec22b330",
                                "text": "In Miscellaneous Reflections, however, Shaftesbury's approach underwent a significant change. Although the miscellanist persona's presence there is felt in a relatively small part of the work, his ironic celebration of the modern art of patchwork wit and his other manufactured ironies recall Swift's satiric ploys in A Tale of a Tub."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
